WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.670
In the sixth episode of Star Trek: Picard,
Jean-Luc Picard and the crew of the La Sirena

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.760
reach the Borg Artifact, and Soji finally
begins to learn the truth about who and what

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.620
she is.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.220
From Voyager nods to visuals pulled straight
from Deep Space Nine, here are all the Easter

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.720
eggs and references in "The Impossible Box."

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.240
Early on in "The Impossible Box," as the La
Sirena nears the former Borg cube now known

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:25.510
as "The Artifact," Picard instructs the computer
in his study to search for the terms "the

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.880
Artifact," "treaty," and "the Borg."

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.160
When he sits down to reluctantly scroll through
the results, several of the images on which

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.940
Picard chooses to linger are easily identifiable.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.760
There's the ex-Borg, Hugh, both when he first
came aboard the Enterprise on The Next Generation,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.181
and then again as he appears in Picard; several
shots of ex-Borg in the midst of their reclamation

00:00:43.181 --> 00:00:46.300
procedures; and of course, Picard as Locutus
of Borg.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.060
But there are also several other quick glimpses
of search results you may not have recognized.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.020
The first was a shot of a Federation ship
firing on a Borg cube and seemingly doing

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.740
some damage, which could have been from the
battle at the opening of Star Trek: First

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.260
Contact.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.520
The next image is of the Romulan Continuing
Committee, which is taken straight from the

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.969
Deep Space Nine episode in which a character
becomes entangled in a Tal Shiar plot.

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:12.939
There is also a brief image of the Paris skyline
in the year 2372, which was taken from the

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.880
Deep Space Nine episode "To the Death."

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.159
In that episode, the DS9 crew has to destroy
an Iconian Gateway, a technological portal

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:24.549
which could instantly transport people over
enormous distances, which is very similar

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:28.630
to the device Picard and Soji use to escape
the Romulans at the end of "The Impossible

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.120
Box."

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.020
As the La Sirena nears the Borg Artifact,
Raffi turns on the charm to contact her old

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.320
friend and Starfleet contact, Emmy, requesting
diplomatic credentials for Picard.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.780
But Emmy refuses at first, saying that the
Romulans are in a "250-year bad mood."

00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:47.139
If you're wondering what happened to the Romulans
two and a half centuries ago to make them

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:52.540
so cranky, well, that's about the time they
first came in contact with humans in 2152.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.800
Just a few years later, in 2156, the Earth-Romulan
war began, which only lasted for four years,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.060
but is still having repercussions over 200
years into the future.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.320
The end of the war was marked by the establishment
of the Romulan neutral zone in 2160, and led

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.540
directly to the formation of the United Federation
of Planets in 2161.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.800
Given the Romulans' long-running tension with
the Federation, it's no wonder that they're

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:16.940
still in a bad mood.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.120
When Hugh mentions in "The Impossible Box"
that the former Borg on the Artifact refer

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.480
to themselves as "xBs," Picard wonders out
loud at the reason for renaming themselves.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:26.480
Hugh explains,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.500
"A new name can be the first step to a new
identity.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.920
I learned that on the Enterprise all those
years ago."

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.000
The lesson Hugh is referring to took place
in the episode where he first appeared on

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.159
The Next Generation, titled "I Borg.”

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.880
At first, Hugh doesn't see himself as an individual,
but as a member of the Collective, referring

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.500
to himself only as "we," and telling the crew
that he is "Third of Five."

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.500
However, after Geordi and Beverly explain
the purpose of names to him, he decides he

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.580
wants one for himself.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.480
"Do I have a name?"

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:55.840
"Do you want one?"

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:57.760
"A name."

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.520
Adopting his own name marks the beginning
of Hugh's ability to think of himself in terms

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.959
of "I," not "we," and ultimately leads to
his lifelong mission to free and rehabilitate

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:07.760
other Borg.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.659
In part, Hugh's job as the Executive Director
of the Borg Reclamation Project involves him

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:16.890
overseeing the rehabilitation efforts of the
traumatized ex-Borg who are trying to recuperate

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.110
from their assimilation.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.251
However, as he shares with Picard, even after
the xBs have gone through reclamation, they

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.400
still aren't accepted by free society.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.020
Hugh says that the xBs remain the most hated
people in the galaxy, just as helpless and

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.940
enslaved as before, but that now, their queen
is a Romulan.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.580
This is a clear nod to the Borg Queen that
Picard himself encountered in Star Trek: First

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:37.080
Contact.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.680
The Queen was the one who directed the Borg's
actions, and she revealed in First Contact

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.560
that it was her idea to assimilate Picard
as "Locutus."

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:49.200
Rather than have just another nameless Borg
drone, the Queen had hoped Picard would serve

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:49.880
as her counterpart.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.260
However, Picard's resistance proved not to
be futile after all, forcing the Queen to

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.360
abandon her plan.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.420
When Picard finds Soji alive on the Artifact,
it's great news for the two of them, but not

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.430
so good for Raffi, who had placed a bet with
Rios that Picard's target would already be

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:04.920
dead.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.520
"You owe me two strips of latinum.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.260
The twin's still alive."

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.280
While latinum has popped up here and there
across multiple Star Trek shows, it was most

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.870
frequently mentioned in Deep Space Nine as
the preferred currency of the Ferengi Alliance,

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.260
and specifically of Quark, the Ferengi bartender
on the space station.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.200
Latinum is often referenced in the Ferengi
Rules of Acquisition, around which Ferengi

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.900
society is structured.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.200
"Rule of Acquisition number 102."

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:35.620
"Good lad!"

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.540
When Picard and Hugh enter Soji's quarters,
a voice can be heard in the background announcing

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:46.139
that "Sectors 5-8 through 5-21 are temporarily
closed due to detected chronometric activity."

00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:50.130
In Star Trek: First Contact, the controlled
emission of chronometric particles is what

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.840
allowed the Borg to travel back in time and
assimilate Earth, which forced the Enterprise

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.260
crew to chase after them and re-establish
the original timeline.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.220
But in addition to the chronometric activity,
the specific sectors that are mentioned may

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.760
also have a hidden meaning.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.310
In the fifth season of Star Trek: The Next
Generation, episode 8 and episode 21 were

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.150
both directed by Cliff Bole.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.449
While neither of these episodes feels particularly
relevant to "The Impossible Box," the same

00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:17.660
cannot be said of two other Bole-directed
episodes of The Next Generation: "The Best

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.520
of Both Worlds," parts I and II.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:23.990
Those, of course, are the episodes in which
Picard is assimilated by the Borg and becomes

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.860
Locutus, an event which was heavily referenced
in "The Impossible Box."

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.039
When Picard, Soji, Hugh, and Elnor find themselves
trapped in the former Borg Queen's chamber,

00:05:33.039 --> 00:05:36.729
Hugh is able to use his Collective memories
of the area to grant them access to the Queen's

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:38.220
failsafe escape route.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:43.040
It turns out the Queen has a spatial trajector,
an instant teleportation device with a wide

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.240
range, which Hugh says was built from technology
the Borg acquired after assimilating Sikarians.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.680
Star Trek fans have actually seen a spatial
trajector once before: on the Star Trek: Voyager

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.780
first season episode "Prime Factors."

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.300
In the episode, Captain Janeway and her crew
encounter the Sikarians, whose teleportation

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:03.480
technology has the potential to cut their
decades-long journey home in half.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000
"I've just been to Alastria and back.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:10.040
Alastria is 40,000 light years away.”

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.300
However, the Sikarians are reluctant to share
their technology due to their own version

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.050
of the Prime Directive, which prohibits them
from interfering in the development of less

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:19.420
advanced cultures.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.240
Sadly, it seems that the technology the Sikarians
were so protective of in Voyager has since

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.020
been taken out of their hands by force.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.980
Although Picard was certainly grateful for
the escape, he'd probably be the first to

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.100
agree that the cost was too high.

